From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] crypto: riscv - GHASH for the T-Head C906

GHASH for lib/crypto on T-Head cores (detected by XTheadVector), such as the
C906 of the SG2002. The C906 multiplier takes about four cycles per
mul/mulhu, unpipelined, once an operand has bits above 31, and one pipelined
cycle when both fit in 32 bits; the generic code multiplies 64-bit operands.
Here the 128x128 carryless product is nine 32x32 ones (two levels of
Karatsuba), each sixteen multiplies of operands with their bits four apart,
all below 2^32, which also makes the multiply timing independent of the data.

Two functions: a scalar one, and one that hashes five blocks per group, four
in XTheadVector lanes (e32, vmul/vmulhu for the two halves of each product,
key powers H^5 to H^2) and the fifth in scalar registers, the two instruction
streams interleaved, with one reduction per group. ghash_preparekey()
precomputes the key operands in struct ghash_key. The vector function runs
between kernel_vector_begin() and kernel_vector_end() for at least five
blocks where may_use_simd(); the rest, and other contexts, use the scalar
one. The assembly is generated, executed against a bit-serial GHASH and its
vector encodings checked against GNU as by
firmware/cpu/go-crypto-c906/gen_ghash_linux.py.

CRYPTO_LIB_GF128HASH_ARCH now also defaults to y with RISCV_ISA_XTHEADVECTOR;
the Zvkg backend keeps its previous conditions and takes precedence.

